'''Lathrianism''' is a {{W|Nontrinitarianism|nontrinitarian}} Christian movement founded in the early 19th century in the [[United Kingdom of Jarin and Itegar]] (modern [[Jarin]]). Emerging from a broader Christian awakening in the region, Lathrianism teaches that {{W|Jesus}} is the sole manifestation of God, rejecting the {{W|Trinity}}. The faith is distinguished by its millenarian expectation of Jesus's return during a great war, a belief that shaped its history and contributed to its decline after the [[Great Adonian War]].

Lathrianism emphasises forgiveness as a central spiritual principle — a teaching that influenced the political thought of [[Manaa Kadar]], whose mother was a Lathrian. The faith's adherents are known as '''Lathrians'''.

== Beliefs ==

=== Nontrinitarianism ===

Lathrianism is {{W|Nontrinitarianism|nontrinitarian}}, holding that {{W|Jesus}} is the one true God manifest in the flesh, rather than the second person of a triune Godhead. This theology resembles {{W|Oneness Pentecostalism}}, rejecting the doctrine of the {{W|Trinity}} as unbiblical and emphasising direct relationship with Jesus as the fullness of deity.

=== Millenarianism ===

The central prophetic belief of Lathrianism is that Jesus will return to earth during a great war. Lathrian eschatology holds that this war will be global in scale and will precede the establishment of God's kingdom on earth. The identification of specific conflicts with this prophecy became a defining feature — and ultimately a source of crisis — for the faith.

=== Forgiveness ===

Lathrianism places exceptional emphasis on {{W|forgiveness}} as a spiritual and practical principle. Adherents are taught that forgiveness must be extended unconditionally, even to those who have caused great harm, and that the capacity to forgive reflects the believer's union with Christ. This teaching influenced secular thought beyond the faith: [[Manaa Kadar]], whose mother [[Amran Kadar]] was a Lathrian, incorporated a principle of atonement into his political ideology, holding that capitalists who recognised their injustice and surrendered their privileges could be forgiven and reintegrated into the socialist state.

== History ==

=== Origins ===

Lathrianism emerged during a Christian awakening in the [[United Kingdom of Jarin and Itegar]] in the early 19th century. The kingdom, located in southeastern [[Kaftia]], had a Christian population influenced by both Orthodox traditions and newer Protestant movements spreading from [[Illypnia]].

The movement was founded by '''[[Lathri]]''' (c. 1790–1865), a religious figure whose origins and early life are poorly documented. Lathri preached a Jesus-centric theology that rejected Trinitarian doctrine and emphasised direct spiritual experience over institutional church authority. His teachings attracted followers who were disillusioned with established churches and drawn to the promise of Christ's imminent return.

=== Spread in southern Kaftia ===

Lathrianism was one of several revivalist Christian movements that spread across [[southern Kaftia]] in the 19th century. The period saw a wave of religious enthusiasm that produced multiple new denominations and sects, many emphasising personal spiritual experience, millenarian expectation, and rejection of established church hierarchies.

In [[Burawa]], Lathrianism arrived as part of this broader religious ferment. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] had lost credibility among some segments of the population due to its alignment with the landowning elite, creating space for alternative Christian movements. Lathrian missionaries found receptive audiences among those seeking a more personal, less hierarchical faith. The Burawi Catholic Church opposed the new movement, but religious protections enacted in the 1800s prevented outright persecution.

Lathrianism never achieved mass popularity in southern Kaftia, remaining a minority faith alongside other revivalist denominations. Its influence exceeded its numbers, however, through the prominence of individual adherents such as [[Amran Kadar]], whose faith influenced her son [[Manaa Kadar]]'s political ideology.

=== Great Adonian War ===

{{main|Great Adonian War}}

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1948) was widely interpreted by Lathrians as the fulfilment of the prophecy of Jesus's return during a great war. The global scale of the conflict, the bombardment of civilian populations, and the involvement of nations across [[Adonia]] appeared to match Lathrian eschatological expectations. Many adherents believed that Jesus would return before or shortly after the war's conclusion.

The war ended in 1948 without the anticipated return. Lathrian leadership subsequently offered reinterpretations of the prophecy: that the war had been a great war but not the Great War of prophecy; that Jesus had returned spiritually rather than physically; or that the signs had been misread. These explanations failed to stem a significant decline in membership. Many former Lathrians joined other Christian denominations, became secular, or embraced the socialist ideology that had gained prestige from the war.

=== Modern era ===

Lathrianism survives as a minor religion in the present day, with approximately 100,000 adherents worldwide. The faith remains strongest in [[Kaftia]], particularly in [[Jarin]] and surrounding regions. In [[Burawa]], Lathrianism is a small minority faith, its historical significance out of proportion to its current numbers.

== Practice ==

Lathrian worship is characterised by simplicity and emotional expressiveness. Services typically include extended prayer, communal singing, and preaching focused on the person of Jesus. The faith does not maintain a hierarchical clergy; local congregations are led by elders or lay preachers.

Sacramental practice varies by congregation, but most Lathrians observe {{W|baptism}} in the name of Jesus (rather than in the Trinitarian formula) and some form of {{W|Communion|commemorative meal}}.

== Demographics ==

Lathrianism is a minority faith in the modern era. Estimates place the total number of adherents at approximately 100,000, concentrated primarily in [[southern Kaftia]]. The faith remains strongest in [[Jarin]], with smaller communities in [[Burawa]] and among diaspora populations.

